+# Attempt account
+
+The predecessor solves a fixed-target cofactor-capacity problem and proves real
+same-grade failure families. The current attachment gives a finite flat
+rank-eight signed completion of the literal-root quartic. The question was
+whether that completion supplies a new integral return or only a transported
+state.
+
+The exact arithmetic specialization gives two different collision patterns:
+E has a p-adic pair and M a p-adic triple. Newton interpolation and the original
+eta scale compute normalization indices p^2 and p^9, their conductor ideals,
+and the actual actions on the finite defect modules. A direct two-variable
+congruence calculation gives the sharp E boundary obstruction at p^3.
+
+The first rational-control construction used CRT to avoid a chosen finite set
+of denominator primes and place a pole at another. It is proved and preserved
+in crt_control_note.tex and replayed, but a simpler construction was then found:
+u=a*l at the R=3 shell passes both numerical gates and stays within 0<u<a^2.
+It fails the original divisor box in exactly one prime occurrence, while all
+specified finite-place denominator tests, original-prime local data and real
+bounds pass. The same primes have independently supplied genuine endpoint
+solutions. This precisely separates the new completion from global exponent
+availability, without claiming a conjecture counterexample.
+
+A manual cross-check of the displayed numerical control against the generated
+JSON corrected the M numerator to2489709608 and its local point description to
+E0/M4 before this release. The main verifier now tests those printed values
+explicitly. This correction does not change any formula or theorem.
+
+What survives: full explicit integral transport and a certificate-level boundary
+for using the attached construction. What does not follow: an initial integer
+ES state at arbitrary p. That remains the Turn7 task. The available capacity
+crossing at p825241 is used only after its original arithmetic word is supplied.
